    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## 10. Key EDA Takeaways\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Finding | Implication |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"|---------|-------------|\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Amounts are log-normally distributed | Log-transform before computing Z-scores |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Fraud has higher Z-scores (right-shifted) | Z-score is a strong signal |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Night hours have elevated fraud rate | `is_night` is a useful binary feature |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| New counterparty → dramatically higher fraud rate | `is_new_counterparty` is the top binary signal |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Geo-impossible transactions are almost always fraud | Hard rule candidate (near-deterministic) |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Each fraud typology has distinct feature signatures | Multi-model ensemble is justified |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Class imbalance ~2% | Use PR-AUC, NOT accuracy or ROC-AUC as primary metric |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Interview-ready summary\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> *\\\"The EDA revealed four strong signals: (1) Z-score of amount vs. user baseline — fraud transactions are 3–8 standard deviations above normal. (2) New counterparty flag — fraud transactions go to first-time payees 80%+ of the time vs 5% for legitimate. (3) Geographic impossibility — near-certain ATO indicator. (4) Night-hour activity — CNP and ATO disproportionately happen 1–4 AM. I encode all four, plus interaction terms, into the feature vector.\\\"*\"\n",
